WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:17.910 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  after the first few hours of Mass Effect  Andromeda I was discouraged maybe even a  little distraught within that short span  of time I'd already encountered  unconvincing animations bog-standard  missions clunky user interface stilted  dialogue basically every red flag you  hope to avoid when approaching a lengthy  shooter RPG powered equally by action  and story thankfully Andromeda did  improve as I progressed I unlocked  exhilarating new combat options met  characters with deeper appeal than my  initial crew and discovered freely  explorable worlds that finally fulfilled  the series decade olds planet-hopping  promise and yet some of those early  problems persisted throughout and while  I did catch glimmers of Mass Effect's  greatness that shine was often dulled by  lifeless dialogue tedious missions and  even technical shortcomings we thought  it fought find them a new life that is  called damn trillion to its credit  Andromeda boldly abandons the familiar  much like its characters in place of the  iconic Commander Shepard we have writer  the daughter or son of a man chosen to  lead one of four arcs filled with  intergalactic explorers looking to found  colonies in a distant star cluster  several disasters later Reiter inherits  her dad's job and while the moments  leading to and including that scene are  pretty hackneyed the stakes really sink  in once you've reached the Nexus  Andromeda's version of the earlier games  Citadel here you discover the other  three arcs have gone missing and that  the Nexus which arrived ahead of the arc  has suffered every setback imaginable  from growing food shortages to a  veritable Civil War when leadership in  shambles the sudden arrival of an arc  immediately lands rider in an  uncomfortable position of power in  practice the scenario felt more  believable than typical you are the  chosen one cliches I understood why  those characters would look to me and  felt the weight of their desperation so  when the Nexus gradually sprang to life  as I started fixing problems I felt  genuinely accomplished an hour ago that  was all dark both the power from Eurasia  you're keeping the lights on you have my  vote in parallel with this more broadly  focused narrative which encompasses much  of the side contents the central  storyline revolves around an evil alien  race and it's delusional narcissistic  leader who opposes more of an immediate  threat than food shortages he is less  one-dimensional than he initially seems  but the plot is largely predictable in a  mindless blockbuster sort of way the two  stories intersect occasionally and both  pay off in the end  truthfully Andromeda story problems stem  more from delivery than from plot the  vast majority of in dramatist characters  are just dole your opinions more  important what do you think of your  people and conversations rarely delve  deeper than arduous get to know you  small talk no one yells or cries or  expresses any measurable emotion at any  point even romance options feel stilted  there's something unique about you  uneasy cruel but somehow profound we're  still your agency in these conversations  is limited  sure you can periodically select from up  to four dialogue options but these  frequently boil down to be optimistic or  be realistic not a huge difference and  regardless of what you pick your inputs  only rarely impact the outcome I can  only recall one major decision that had  serious repercussions and even that felt  contrived they'd also paled in  comparison to be memorably gut-wrenching  choices forced on me in the original  games what do you think military  outposts are scientific in fairness  Andromeda did sometimes surprised me  with poignant moments like my crew  comforting me in a dark hour and a  conversation with my partner AI about  the meaning of life  the game just buries these gems under  hours of empty or even cringe-worthy  interactions we discovered the remnant  and breach their vault and didn't get  killed overcame radiation poisoning a  number of times and didn't die  confronted by Kat at every turn once  again did not get killed thankfully I  didn't have to dig as deep to find the  things Andromeda does well it's worlds  for example are breathtaking to behold  and exciting to explore you eventually  uncover four mini open worlds as well as  smaller standalone areas like an  overgrown jungle outpost and your own  ships The Tempest the four major maps  are sizeable and offer drastically  different environments and hazards  they're also filled with NPCs chat up  and side missions to undertake you could  end up solving murders in a pirate port  setting at a Krogan fighting pits or  unlocking secrets in ancient yet  hyper-advanced vaults or you could just  wheel around in your nomad the galaxy is  vast and varied and that's worth being  excited about I also fell in love with  the combat especially later in the game  the core shooting mechanics feels  stronger here than anywhere else in the  series and the flexibility of the  progression system let me cherry pick  cool towers rather than locking me in to  a set character class I ended up  building well a space ninja basically I  could use tech to cloak myself biotics  to charge my enemies the shield buffing  sword to heal damage and the standard  jump Jets to dart away again the results  were consistently frantic and fun though  there are plenty of other options as  well  I enjoy basically everything I  experimented with even if most enemies  proved to be predictable adversaries  combats one major flaw is the crafting  system I would call it more of a missed  opportunity than a problem but crafting  is often the only way to get the weapons  and armor you actually want which means  hours of scanning objects to occur  research points and many headaches  dealing with the messy UI good luck  trying to directly compare weapon stats  the crafting and loadout stations are  also at opposite ends of your ship which  frequently forced me to run back and  forth to get things done I felt deeply  satisfied when I finally completed my  perfect loadout  I'm not sure it was worth the energy  crafting isn't Andromeda's biggest time  waster though that would be it's tedious  mission far too many open world quests  even some that feel important or come  packaged in an interesting premise  devolve into multi-step go here hit a  button errands there's always another  nap point somewhere across the map or an  NPC who needs exactly three items or a  crucial data pad that's unexpectedly  missing when you arrive these missions  desperately needed some editing or at  least a wider variety of gameplay  scenarios if you can collect the  necessary resources from Aradia  it should work the campaign and crew  loyalty missions provides better crafted  experiences but there's no avoiding at  least some of the unimaginative tedium  there is plenty to do outside of  missions however Andromeda includes a  somewhat convoluted meta game that  challenges you to raise planet's  viability levels by establishing  outposts and completing other quests you  can also hunt for memory triggers left  by your father that eventually reveal a  few interesting secrets and then there's  mining collectibles space travel strike  teams there's even a decent cooperative  multiplayer horde mode individually  these elements don't add much but  collectively they do round out the  sci-fi fantasy  unfortunately there's a dark cloud  hanging over all of this technical  issues yes the facial animations really  don't look great but the problems run  deeper on ps4 the framerate was all over  the place both in and out of combat on  both ps4 and PC I encountered several  audio issues most notably multiple lines  of dialogue playing at the same time  covering each other  I also saw several random glitches like  characters that fail to load during  conversations and enemies that fell into  the level geometry none of these issues  rendered the game unplayable but they  were noticeable and pervasive in many  ways Andromeda feels like a vision half  fulfilled it contains a dizzying amount  of content but the quality fluctuates  wildly its worlds in combat shine but  it's writing and missions falter as a  Mass Effect game Andromeda falls well  short of the nuance politics morality  and storytelling of its predecessor yet  even after 65 hours I still plan on  completing a few more quests the game  can't fully escape its shortcomings but  patient explorers can still find a few  stars shining in the darkness but today  whatever our reason  we take the first step toward a new  future for Humanity  today we begin to make our tree yeah  [Music]  you

